    ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÙ

    VTYPE is a fast TYPE replacement with ANSI emulation.

    Because VTYPE writes directly into the video RAM, it does not support
    output redirection, however input may be redirected, i.e. piped from
    an other program. ANSI.SYS (or equivalent) is not required.

    VTYPE should handle ANSI sequences like ANSI.SYS does (except keyboard
    reassignments and mode changes) - with the following enhancement:

        [2J    blanks the screen - but if, instead of "2", the ASCII
                value of a character (greater than 32) is used, the screen
                is filled with this character. You may also use the
                character itself, enclosed in (single or double) quotes.
                Examples:

        [177J  fills the screen with "±" (ASCII 177 pattern)
        ["±"J  will do the same


    VTYPE recognizes the current screen size (total lines) and width.

    Command options:

        /C      Clear screen with color found at current cursor position

        /P[n]   Pause after n lines on screen and at end of file (*)
                (default n = screen lines -1).
                VTYPE counts the lines on the screen (not in the file)

        /S      Slow (about 18 lines per second), any key pauses (*)

        /An     pause on ASCII Code (n: decimal value) occurring in file

        /L50    switch from 25 to 50 lines mode
                pausing at end, before switching back to 25 lines

        /J      If the size of a line is exactly equal to the screen width
                (normally 80 characters), VTYPE will not make an additional
                line feed - as TYPE does.

        /X      ANSI off


    (*) Pauses are suspended until the bottom of screen is reached (!)

    Pause keys:
        ESC to abort, cursor down to pause again after next line.
        Any other key continues.

